# React and Next.js Standards for Web App

When working in `apps/web`:

## Architecture and Boundaries

- Prefer Server Components by default, and only use `'use client'` when interactivity or browser-only APIs are required.
- Keep components focused: UI components should ideally be coming from the `packages/react-common` package. Otherwise (when domain logic is needed) they live in `src/components`, feature-specific logic in `src/features/<feature>`.
- Avoid putting business logic in presentational components; move data shaping and side effects into feature-level actions/queries/hooks.

## Data and State

- Use server-side data fetching for initial page data whenever practical.
- Keep client state minimal and local; avoid global state unless multiple distant parts of the UI truly share the same state.
- Keep async flows explicit with typed return values and clear error handling paths.

## Component APIs

- Design components with small, composable props instead of boolean-prop proliferation.
- Prefer composition patterns for reusable UI primitives (slots/children/compound patterns) over large monolithic components.
- Keep props and internal state strictly typed. Do not use `any`.

## UX, Accessibility, and Styling

- Build accessible defaults: semantic HTML, keyboard support, and meaningful labels for interactive controls.
- Use consistent loading, empty, and error states for async UI.
- Follow the established styling system in the app and avoid one-off styling patterns that reduce reuse.

## Skills Usage (Only When Applicable)

- Use `.cursor/skills/next-best-practices/SKILL.md` for Next.js App Router patterns, Server/Client Component boundaries, route handlers, metadata, and async API usage.
- Use `.cursor/skills/vercel-react-best-practices/SKILL.md` for React/Next.js performance-sensitive work (rendering, bundle size, data loading, or interaction latency).
- Use `.cursor/skills/vercel-composition-patterns/SKILL.md` when refactoring component APIs, reducing prop complexity, or building reusable component systems.
- Use `.cursor/skills/web-design-guidelines/SKILL.md` only for explicit UI/UX/accessibility/design audits and reviews.
- Do not load skills by default; select them only when the current task clearly matches the skill's use case.
